![Unit-02 against the Mass Production Evangelions, the Lance of Longinus crossing the sky above a scorched battlefield](/posters/the-end-of-evangelion-2.jpg)

The final scene of End of Evangelion takes place in this surreal environment on a beach with the 3 central characters each with their own representation on what the ending typifies. Rei, the symbol of the Instrumentality project, looms large in the far distance in the ocean, seeming a Goddess among humanity, and with a sense of inevitability from the scenes she's in. Asuka, the embodiment of humanity's will to fight, sits with her hands on her knees on the beach, after escaping death one too many times. And our main protagonist, Shinji, standing between the two, at the boundary of where the sand meets the ocean, an illustration of the tug of war in his mind. To me, this dynamic and this endless battle in Shinji's mind is what makes End of Evangelion so haunting, but so beautiful and incredibly profound.



![Shinji’s bandaged hand closing around Asuka’s throat, her face turned up towards him](/posters/the-end-of-evangelion-1.jpg)

Does the inevitability of pain and loss make life worth living? Throughout the anime and the film, Shinji is repeatedly hollowed out. In Yui, his mother, we find his first wound, the mother that he never had. This absence hangs over his head as we progress through the story, and we see how he reaches out to Misato to find some semblance of normalcy and love where his father couldn't provide. Yui represents this lack of humanity remaining in Shinji. Conversely, Asuka is the embodiment of humanity in his mind. She's proof that feeling anything is more human than numbness, and there is no more proof of this than the final scene of Shinji strangling her. He needs to know that she exists not just in his mind, but in the real world, a world that no longer feels familiar, a world that seems to lack free will. That bridge from Asuka is what brings him back and shows him what's normal.



![Two crucified Evangelion units hanging over a red horizon, Shinji collapsed on the ground below](/posters/the-end-of-evangelion-3.jpg)

Instrumentality represents the death of free will and free emotion, it traps people into shells of themselves but ensures no pain or loss. Shinji rejects this, understanding of the value in himself and his relationships with others and that, in one of the most powerful moments in the show, "Its okay for me to be here." Its the scene that is the crux of what End of Evangelion represents. That life is worth it, and so are you, no matter how terrible or how much pain there exists. There is beauty in the ups and downs in life. "Its okay for me to be here" is not Shinji's triumph. Its just enough for him to keep going.
